WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:09:46.857 --> 00:09:56.857
[MUSIC]

00:10:03.644 --> 00:10:05.943
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:10:05.943 --> 00:10:15.943
[MUSIC]

00:12:35.058 --> 00:12:43.741
[APPLAUSE].

00:12:43.741 --> 00:12:49.856
&gt;&gt; Graduates, Phil Knight, honored guests,
faculty, staff,

00:12:49.856 --> 00:12:55.747
families, and friends, welcome to the 2014
graduation

00:12:55.747 --> 00:13:02.225
ceremony at Stanford University's Graduate
School of Business.

00:13:02.225 --> 00:13:12.301
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE].

00:13:12.301 --> 00:13:14.710
&gt;&gt; What does your graduation signify?

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:20.570
As you know, studying at the GSB means so

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:23.170
much more than mastering the concepts of
management.

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:27.730
It is as much about personal
transformation.

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:34.430
As you sit before us, eager to receive
your diplomas, I encourage you

00:13:34.430 --> 00:13:38.990
to think about how you have changed and
grown during your time here.

00:13:38.990 --> 00:13:45.880
Your graduation today celebrates all that
you have done and become.

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:50.200
And your readiness to have a dramatic and

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:56.130
positive impact on the organizations you
will lead, manage,

00:13:56.130 --> 00:14:02.140
and found, and through those
organizations, the communities they serve.

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:10.300
What you have accomplished in your time at
the GSB you have not accomplished alone.

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:15.970
You have learned and received help and
guidance, mentorship and

00:14:15.970 --> 00:14:19.520
support from many quarters.

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:22.390
All of which are represented here today.

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:28.380
First, you are sitting shoulder to
shoulder with your classmates.

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:30.260
As you have throughout your time here.

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:37.862
Think how much you have learned from one
another in squads,

00:14:37.862 --> 00:14:46.524
clubs, study trips, talk, touchy feely, or
just in quiet conversation together.

00:14:46.524 --> 00:14:51.620
You have forged bonds that will become
lifelong friendships which

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:55.358
will nurture and sustain you in the years
ahead.

00:14:55.358 --> 00:15:01.359
Take a moment to show your appreciation
for

00:15:01.359 --> 00:15:06.860
one another [APPLAUSE] Sitting behind me

00:15:06.860 --> 00:15:13.361
on the stage is a group of faculty who
represent

00:15:13.361 --> 00:15:20.170
the more than 200 tenure line faculty.

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:25.990
And practitioners who have been your
teachers, coachers, mentors,

00:15:25.990 --> 00:15:30.920
study trip companions, career advisers,
and so much more.

00:15:32.140 --> 00:15:36.815
I would ask the faculty to stand so that
your students can show their appreciation.

00:15:36.815 --> 00:15:46.815
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE].

00:15:53.190 --> 00:15:58.770
&gt;&gt; And at various locations throughout the
amphitheater are staff from the MBA,

00:15:58.770 --> 00:16:01.770
MSX, and PhD programs.

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:05.190
Without their tireless efforts on your
behalf,

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:08.140
none of us would be celebrating here
today.

00:16:08.140 --> 00:16:11.915
I ask the staff to stand, wherever they
are, and be recognized.

00:16:11.915 --> 00:16:17.696
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE].

00:16:17.696 --> 00:16:24.039
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:16:24.039 --> 00:16:31.060
I have left the most important for last.

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:35.990
Each of you has been supported by family
and

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:38.860
friends as you will throughout your lives.

00:16:39.910 --> 00:16:45.268
They've provided encouragement,
validation, love, and

00:16:45.268 --> 00:16:49.500
yes, in many cases, financial support,
too.

00:16:51.460 --> 00:16:54.844
While we will hand your diplomas to you,
you know that in

00:16:54.844 --> 00:16:59.884
parentheses after your name, belong the
names of those who have nurtured you, and

00:16:59.884 --> 00:17:02.660
supported you along the way.

00:17:02.660 --> 00:17:07.880
This is your opportunity to stand and say
thank you to your friends and

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:13.107
family, who are celebrating your mutual
accomplishment with you.

00:17:13.107 --> 00:17:19.555
[APPLAUSE] Most days during your

00:17:19.555 --> 00:17:28.483
time in the new Knight Management Center,

00:17:28.483 --> 00:17:35.179
you have walked through the town

00:17:35.179 --> 00:17:39.891
square on your way to or

00:17:39.891 --> 00:17:46.339
from class and have walked by,

00:17:46.339 --> 00:17:52.539
around or through the replica

00:17:52.539 --> 00:17:59.483
of an athletics track which holds

00:17:59.483 --> 00:18:07.420
an inscribed quote from Phil Knight.

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:14.400
The quote begins, there comes a time in
every life.

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:18.070
Where the past recedes and the future
opens.

00:18:19.190 --> 00:18:23.320
It's that moment when you turn to face the
unknown.

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:33.240
Stepping out of that athletics track into
the GSB, are a pair of sneaker prints.

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:38.870
&gt;&gt; From a pair of filth Nike's, and let's
all admit it,

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:46.350
in the privacy of this intimate gathering,
most of us have at one time or

00:18:46.350 --> 00:18:52.610
another, put our own feet into those
footprints.

00:18:52.610 --> 00:18:54.080
And stepped into the GSB.

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:59.700
I thank you for the myriad ways in which
you have stepped into the GSB,

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:04.390
and enriched it with your presence during
your time here.

00:19:06.840 --> 00:19:10.620
Now you face one of those moments that
Phil talks about in the inscription,

00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:15.580
where the future opens, and you turn to
face the unknown.

00:19:17.350 --> 00:19:20.410
And at the end of the stage here today,

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:25.420
we have put down another pair of
footprints to encourage you all to

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:30.910
think about how you will step from the GSB
into the future.

00:19:32.650 --> 00:19:35.060
I have three hopes for you as you take
that step.

00:19:36.670 --> 00:19:39.510
First, take a huge stride.

00:19:41.418 --> 00:19:45.260
At the GSB, we have not just encouraged
you to dream but

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:49.290
try to instill in you the courage to
pursue those dreams.

00:19:49.290 --> 00:19:53.330
We have surrounded you by classmates who
can help you to nurture them.

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:57.920
And we have provided you with an education
that gives you the knowledge and

00:19:57.920 --> 00:20:00.460
insight to guide you in your quest.

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:05.960
We have done this because we think that
the biggest problems in the world,

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:11.464
whether in education, global poverty,
health care,

00:20:11.464 --> 00:20:14.894
the environment, water, governance or

00:20:14.894 --> 00:20:20.600
business are at their core, business
leadership and management challenges.

00:20:22.780 --> 00:20:27.640
There are big and important challenges,
and the world needs change agents,

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:32.450
who are willing to tackle them with
passion, and with innovative ideas.

00:20:34.430 --> 00:20:40.240
Changing lives, organizations, and the
world requires that you dream big.

00:20:43.770 --> 00:20:46.380
Second, walk with confidence.

00:20:48.050 --> 00:20:53.000
It takes courage to lead and to innovate
in order to bring about real change.

00:20:54.140 --> 00:20:56.610
Don't shy away from living a life of
impact and

00:20:56.610 --> 00:21:01.210
meaning, even if that isn't always their
easiest road to walk.

00:21:03.250 --> 00:21:04.930
Changing lives, organizations, and

00:21:04.930 --> 00:21:10.168
the world requires not just ambition, but
purpose as well.

00:21:10.168 --> 00:21:15.590
Third, walk together.

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:22.320
There's an old African saying which goes,
walk together, walk far.

00:21:24.010 --> 00:21:26.340
Stay as tightly connected as you are
today,

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:29.960
and continue to support one another in
your aspirations.

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:35.160
And come back regularly and stay connected
to all of us.

00:21:37.020 --> 00:21:41.050
Changing lives, organizations, and the
world is a team sport.

00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:48.060
So as you walk off this stage today
diploma in hand and

00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:51.890
you step into the future, take a big
stride.

00:21:53.140 --> 00:21:56.210
Walk with confidence, and walk together.

00:21:58.240 --> 00:22:03.470
We are looking forward to following your
journeys as you step out of the GSB today.

00:22:07.910 --> 00:22:13.970
Our graduation speaker today is an alumnus
who has not just taken big strides

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:19.955
himself, but who, who has also created the
footwear with which to do it.

00:22:19.955 --> 00:22:23.620
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:22:23.620 --> 00:22:27.450
&gt;&gt; Phil Knight graduated from the Stanford
GSB with an MBA in 1962,

00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:32.140
and went on to co-found Nike with Bill
Bowerman,

00:22:32.140 --> 00:22:37.050
his former running coach, at his undergrad
alma mater, the University of Oregon.

00:22:38.760 --> 00:22:41.990
You may think Phil turned to making shoes
instead,

00:22:41.990 --> 00:22:43.838
because he wasn't any good at running in
them.

00:22:43.838 --> 00:22:46.840
Not so.

00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:51.270
Phil clocked a personal best of four
minutes, ten seconds in the mile.

00:22:54.910 --> 00:22:55.940
Phil is a great guy.

00:22:57.180 --> 00:22:58.940
And I don't just mean that in the narrow,
but

00:22:58.940 --> 00:23:03.970
very impressive sense of someone who co,
co-founded and lead one of

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:08.360
the most successful influential and iconic
brands in the world.

00:23:09.910 --> 00:23:11.140
Phil leads with character.

00:23:12.520 --> 00:23:17.020
He's a man of integrity who has the
courage to speak his mind.

00:23:17.020 --> 00:23:18.160
And he speaks from the heart.

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:20.240
I love that about him.

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:25.900
Phil is also a thoughtful and generous
philanthropist.

00:23:27.120 --> 00:23:30.600
In addition to providing the lead gift of
$105 million to

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:34.970
enable the building of the Knight
Management Center and

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:39.750
being a great supporter of athletics at
the University of Oregon, Phil and

00:23:39.750 --> 00:23:45.651
his wife Penny, last year pledged $500
million in matching funds.

00:23:45.651 --> 00:23:52.314
That's $500 million.

00:23:52.314 --> 00:23:58.780
[APPLAUSE] Towards the Knight Cancer
Institute at Oregon Health and

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:06.170
Science University, all of us, especially
those who either personally or

00:24:06.170 --> 00:24:12.059
in our families have been touched by that
terrible disease,

00:24:12.059 --> 00:24:18.660
thank Phil and Penny for their generosity
of spirit and resources.

00:24:20.380 --> 00:24:24.055
Please join me in giving Phil Knight a
warm GSB welcome.

00:24:24.055 --> 00:24:32.740
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE].

00:24:32.740 --> 00:24:34.920
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:38.680
Thank you.

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:44.681
Dean Saloner, graduates, family,

00:24:44.681 --> 00:24:50.119
friends, faculty, Dean Joss and

00:24:50.119 --> 00:24:55.558
our honorable Condoleezza Rice,

00:24:55.558 --> 00:25:03.530
I'm sorry that RJ Miller could not be here
today.

00:25:03.530 --> 00:25:08.360
He would be the one person in this
gathering that would refer to

00:25:08.360 --> 00:25:09.945
me as that young man.

00:25:09.945 --> 00:25:14.190
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:25:14.190 --> 00:25:19.380
&gt;&gt; I graduated from this school in 1962,
more than half a century ago.

00:25:19.380 --> 00:25:22.090
It was a time when jet travel was

00:25:22.090 --> 00:25:25.810
just beginning with the introduction of
the Boeing 707.

00:25:25.810 --> 00:25:29.875
There was no Silicon Valley, per se.

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:34.610
There were no ISPACS machines, there was
no internet,

00:25:35.690 --> 00:25:37.970
there were no cell phones, no iPads.

00:25:37.970 --> 00:25:42.740
The latest technology, technological
development was the colored tv.

00:25:42.740 --> 00:25:46.760
There was no such thing as venture
capital.

00:25:46.760 --> 00:25:50.520
The number one company in the world was
General Motors.

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:54.450
The biggest firm on Wall Street was
Merrill Lynch, Pierce, Fenner &amp; Smith.

00:25:55.730 --> 00:26:01.150
Commercial banks were not allowed to
engage in investment banking activities.

00:26:01.150 --> 00:26:03.045
And there was no birth control pill.

00:26:03.045 --> 00:26:05.760
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:08.980
&gt;&gt; There was absolutely nothing in my
journey that has

00:26:08.980 --> 00:26:11.940
any specific application for what awaits
you.

00:26:11.940 --> 00:26:17.980
In so many ways, today's talk could be
called Return of the Dinosaur.

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:26.899
Why, therefore, why, before this, the
greatest

00:26:26.899 --> 00:26:34.685
class ever to graduate from this best
business school on the planet.

00:26:34.685 --> 00:26:35.900
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE].

00:26:35.900 --> 00:26:38.500
&gt;&gt; Why, why did the dean ask me to be
here?

00:26:38.500 --> 00:26:41.025
And my answer is I don't really know.

00:26:41.025 --> 00:26:44.140
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I suppose there may be

00:26:44.140 --> 00:26:50.110
some hope that parts of my journey might
be relevant in attitude and philosophy.

00:26:50.110 --> 00:26:50.610
I hope so.

00:26:51.840 --> 00:26:57.640
But why I, a person who intensely dislikes
public speaking chose to accept,

00:26:57.640 --> 00:26:58.690
is perfectly clear to me.

00:26:59.970 --> 00:27:01.900
The answer is, it is personal.

00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:05.490
For me, it's a rounding of the circle.

00:27:06.530 --> 00:27:08.680
There's a part of me that was born here.

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:14.350
I had come here, at age 22, a bit lost.

00:27:14.350 --> 00:27:18.375
For me, an extrovert was a person who
stared at other people's shoes.

00:27:18.375 --> 00:27:23.770
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:27:23.770 --> 00:27:28.170
&gt;&gt; Shy, insecure, unsure of what I wanted
to do with my life.

00:27:28.170 --> 00:27:29.720
Two years later, I left.

00:27:29.720 --> 00:27:30.670
Much better educated.

00:27:30.670 --> 00:27:35.800
I was still shy and insecure, but I knew
what I wanted to do.

00:27:35.800 --> 00:27:37.980
If only, I could pull it off.

00:27:37.980 --> 00:27:40.680
And that was to bring to life the business
plan written in

00:27:40.680 --> 00:27:42.740
Frank Shellenberger's Entrepreneurship
Class.

00:27:44.170 --> 00:27:49.690
So I returned, 52 years after my own
graduation, to this place,

00:27:49.690 --> 00:27:53.110
this magical place which is an extended
part of me.

00:27:54.210 --> 00:27:59.970
I return to say thank you here where all
the aspiration began.

00:27:59.970 --> 00:28:03.865
In the summer between my first and second
year,

00:28:03.865 --> 00:28:09.330
I'd had a long existential debate with
myself, finally concluding that I would,

00:28:09.330 --> 00:28:13.940
before going to work for 40 years, take
one year to go around the world,

00:28:13.940 --> 00:28:18.350
looking for education, for enlightenment,
looking for myself.

00:28:19.630 --> 00:28:21.510
And then the winter term of my second
year,

00:28:21.510 --> 00:28:25.390
I took that entrepreneurship class, whose
road led to Japan.

00:28:26.740 --> 00:28:31.090
So after putting in my required two weeks
of U.S. Army summer camp at Fort Ord,

00:28:31.090 --> 00:28:35.170
selling my car, saying bon voyage to
parents and sisters,

00:28:35.170 --> 00:28:39.280
I set out with Gary Carter who I met when
we were both living in Crothers Hall.

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:41.808
In September we were ready to go.

00:28:41.808 --> 00:28:46.820
We drove down El Camino to the liquor
store that was the ticket agent for

00:28:46.820 --> 00:28:49.419
Standard Airways, a discount charter
airline.

00:28:50.420 --> 00:28:52.630
For $80, we got on a Convair 880,

00:28:52.630 --> 00:28:58.100
a prop of course, leaving from Moffat
Field, eight hours to Hawaii.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:01.680
We would surf in the mornings and

00:29:01.680 --> 00:29:04.178
sell encyclopedias door to door in the
early evening.

00:29:04.178 --> 00:29:09.620
I'm sure I'm the only graduate in the
history of this school,

00:29:09.620 --> 00:29:13.185
whose first job after graduation was
selling encyclopedias.

00:29:13.185 --> 00:29:17.040
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; After a month of this,

00:29:17.040 --> 00:29:21.120
I was ready to go on, but Gary had met a
girl he liked a little too much to leave.

00:29:21.120 --> 00:29:25.170
So now I'm stuck with a decision, go home,
or not go at all.

00:29:27.140 --> 00:29:28.360
I loved Japan instantly.

00:29:29.380 --> 00:29:33.570
The people were friendly, and many parts
of the countryside scenic.

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:35.940
And the Yen was 3.60 to the dollar.

00:29:35.940 --> 00:29:40.708
So, so many things were affordable, like
hotel rooms, and meals, and

00:29:40.708 --> 00:29:41.891
athletic shoes.

00:29:41.891 --> 00:29:45.503
After a week I'd run my way to Kobe which
was the headquarters of

00:29:45.503 --> 00:29:49.460
Onitsuka Company Limited, manufacturer of
Tiger athletic shoes,

00:29:49.460 --> 00:29:51.778
which I identified as the best quality,

00:29:51.778 --> 00:29:55.482
the ones with the best chance of getting a
piece of the US market.

00:29:55.482 --> 00:29:59.023
I called on the telephone and explained I
was a US businessman in town.

00:29:59.023 --> 00:30:03.085
And I had an interest in distributing
their shoes in the United States.

00:30:03.085 --> 00:30:05.590
I got an appointment.

00:30:05.590 --> 00:30:08.580
I donned my one business suit, Brooks
Brothers,

00:30:08.580 --> 00:30:13.740
with a blue Oxford shirt, black neck tie,
I slipped on my black Bailey loafers, and

00:30:13.740 --> 00:30:16.105
took a taxi to the wrong location.

00:30:16.105 --> 00:30:18.780
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:30:18.780 --> 00:30:19.905
&gt;&gt; I couldn't read the signs.

00:30:19.905 --> 00:30:24.990
[LAUGH]
I'd gone to the show room, they

00:30:24.990 --> 00:30:28.580
wanted me at the manufacturing facility,
completely on the other side of town.

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:33.130
So, I show up a half hour late, I'm
already nervous.

00:30:33.130 --> 00:30:37.090
And even though it was a cool day, I was
sweating heavily.

00:30:37.090 --> 00:30:39.460
I'm met at the door by a 30ish man.

00:30:39.460 --> 00:30:41.980
Ken Miyazaki, who reached me warmly and

00:30:41.980 --> 00:30:45.840
users me through to a conference room in
the back of the building.

00:30:45.840 --> 00:30:49.410
On the way to the room we pass through the
accounting department which has

00:30:49.410 --> 00:30:53.840
about 20 employees, they all stand up and
bow.

00:30:53.840 --> 00:30:58.140
The big business man from the US, [LAUGH]
don't you know?

00:30:58.140 --> 00:30:59.942
My entire assets are on my body.

00:30:59.942 --> 00:31:01.995
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:31:01.995 --> 00:31:04.730
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:04.730 --> 00:31:08.190
&gt;&gt; That business suit, and my around the
world airline ticket.

00:31:08.190 --> 00:31:10.202
It occurred to me that I might not get out
of there alive.

00:31:10.202 --> 00:31:13.450
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; There are a half a dozen Japanese

00:31:13.450 --> 00:31:15.840
businessmen waiting in the conference
room.

00:31:15.840 --> 00:31:19.460
How does a shy, insecure person make a
sales presentation?

00:31:20.480 --> 00:31:22.900
Sell as if your life depended on it.

00:31:22.900 --> 00:31:24.215
Which it sorta did, didn't it.

00:31:24.215 --> 00:31:27.580
[COUGH] But I must confess, when I

00:31:27.580 --> 00:31:31.900
said I sold encyclopedias door-to-door in
Honolulu, it was a little imprecise.

00:31:32.980 --> 00:31:36.500
I called on houses door-to-door trying to
sell encyclopedias,

00:31:36.500 --> 00:31:38.115
I never actually closed a sale.

00:31:38.115 --> 00:31:39.844
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:31:42.310 --> 00:31:43.890
&gt;&gt; After a very awkward beginning,

00:31:43.890 --> 00:31:46.670
we got into the specifics about what was
needed for the US market.

00:31:46.670 --> 00:31:50.980
The talks warm up and eventually become
enthusiastic.

00:31:50.980 --> 00:31:54.900
They have been thinking about getting into
the US market and have several track and

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:58.290
field sample models built on patterns of
the US foot.

00:31:58.290 --> 00:32:00.350
They have an all-purpose model they call
the Limber Up.

00:32:00.350 --> 00:32:02.660
To have a high jump model called the
spring-up,

00:32:02.660 --> 00:32:07.078
and a shot discus shoe they call the
throw-up.

00:32:07.078 --> 00:32:10.230
[COUGH].
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:32:10.230 --> 00:32:11.845
&gt;&gt; I might be able to help them.

00:32:11.845 --> 00:32:16.570
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:32:16.570 --> 00:32:20.120
&gt;&gt; So we end with me placing an order of
15 pair of samples.

00:32:20.120 --> 00:32:24.010
And after I leave, I sit alone on a chair
in the chaotic Osaka airport and

00:32:24.010 --> 00:32:26.550
ask myself again, where am I going?

00:32:26.550 --> 00:32:29.670
I am very excited about the meeting with
[UNKNOWN] and one part says,

00:32:29.670 --> 00:32:31.950
this is exactly what I want.

00:32:31.950 --> 00:32:34.790
I should race home and get this business
going.

00:32:34.790 --> 00:32:38.020
The other said, if you don't go around the
world now, you'll not go for

00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:38.790
four or five decades.

00:32:39.860 --> 00:32:41.510
I flew to Hong Kong.

00:32:41.510 --> 00:32:42.130
Which is a good thing.

00:32:42.130 --> 00:32:44.380
The samples didn't arrive for 14 months.

00:32:44.380 --> 00:32:48.470
When the samples finally came in, I showed
them to my old coach Bill Bowerman,

00:32:48.470 --> 00:32:51.440
who was so impressed he asked to let him
in on the deal.

00:32:51.440 --> 00:32:56.000
We shook hands on a 50 50 partnership, and
each of us put 500 dollars.

00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:58.380
We bought 300 pairs of shoes.

00:32:58.380 --> 00:33:00.154
First year sales were $8,000.

00:33:01.210 --> 00:33:02.300
We made a $250 profit.

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:07.070
In 1964 my life got busy.

00:33:07.070 --> 00:33:09.910
By day I was a CPA for Price Waterhouse.

00:33:09.910 --> 00:33:13.980
My Army reserve requirement takes up two
days, two Tuesday nights a month and

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:17.130
one all-day Sunday plus two weeks in the
summer.

00:33:17.130 --> 00:33:18.880
Dating with mixed success, and

00:33:18.880 --> 00:33:23.100
all the while my real love is that little
company that Bauman and I have started.

00:33:23.100 --> 00:33:28.590
We get to $100,000 in sales, we get to
$500,000, then a million.

00:33:28.590 --> 00:33:33.740
I multitask, I can drive a car, eat a
McDonald's fillet of fish sticks and

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:35.415
read a newspaper all at the same time.

00:33:35.415 --> 00:33:37.120
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:33:37.120 --> 00:33:38.140
&gt;&gt; I was pretty efficient for

00:33:38.140 --> 00:33:40.232
a couple years, until I rear-ended the car
in front of me.

00:33:40.232 --> 00:33:41.695
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:33:41.695 --> 00:33:44.330
&gt;&gt; It took a lot of time to get insurance
companies lined up, and

00:33:44.330 --> 00:33:47.000
my own car repaired, and the cuts on my
forehead fixed.

00:33:47.000 --> 00:33:50.490
So, I don't do that anymore.

00:33:50.490 --> 00:33:54.680
By 1972, we got to $2 million in sales,
with a 3% net profit, but

00:33:54.680 --> 00:33:55.378
it hadn't been easy.

00:33:55.378 --> 00:34:00.806
After all, $500 apiece doesn't provide
much equity, even for $2 million.

00:34:00.806 --> 00:34:03.060
I had tossed in most of my Price
Waterhouse checks for

00:34:03.060 --> 00:34:05.970
four years, but I was spending four days a
week at the bank trying to

00:34:05.970 --> 00:34:08.410
convince them to give us a little more
credit.

00:34:08.410 --> 00:34:11.570
By now, I have quit Price Waterhouse, and
I'm going full time, and

00:34:11.570 --> 00:34:15.440
somewhere in the process my search for
credit put me in touch with [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:15.440 --> 00:34:17.430
The sixth largest Japanese trading
company,

00:34:17.430 --> 00:34:20.580
with annual sales of 100 billion dollars.

00:34:20.580 --> 00:34:23.160
We began developing a positive
relationship.

00:34:23.160 --> 00:34:25.678
Meanwhile, [UNKNOWN] had brought a 32 year
old hotshot,

00:34:25.678 --> 00:34:29.670
[UNKNOWN] with a charge to expand export
sales.

00:34:29.670 --> 00:34:32.146
Export sales were primarily us.

00:34:32.146 --> 00:34:35.178
So, young Mr. [UNKNOWN] brings me the
following offer.

00:34:35.178 --> 00:34:40.930
Sell us 51% of your company at book value,
or we'll set up other distributors,

00:34:40.930 --> 00:34:44.630
regardless of what that piece of paper
that we signed says.

00:34:44.630 --> 00:34:49.700
With com, Katani's ultimatum, I did an
instant cost benefit analysis,

00:34:49.700 --> 00:34:53.255
which led me to this dilemma, how do you
say go to hell in Japanese?

00:34:53.255 --> 00:34:54.614
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:34:55.710 --> 00:35:01.515
&gt;&gt; So there I am, 34 years old, married, a
three-year-old son, 80% mortgage

00:35:01.515 --> 00:35:07.650
on my house, 45 employees, a personal
guarantee of $750,000 company loan,

00:35:07.650 --> 00:35:12.510
inventory going more obsolete by the day,
and no new product to sell.

00:35:14.100 --> 00:35:17.910
We had a team that had taken, that had
been nothing eight years before, and

00:35:17.910 --> 00:35:20.970
built it up to become recognized in the
sporting goods world.

00:35:20.970 --> 00:35:24.450
And that team had designed the three
top-selling Tiger running shoes.

00:35:24.450 --> 00:35:26.350
We had a large pot of coffee and

00:35:26.350 --> 00:35:28.740
the support of the sixth largest Japanese
trading company.

00:35:29.750 --> 00:35:31.170
And that trading company could and

00:35:31.170 --> 00:35:34.580
did introduce us to every shoe factory in
Japan.

00:35:34.580 --> 00:35:36.960
And provided the financing to import those
products.

00:35:38.120 --> 00:35:41.890
But then there was the little matter of
the lawsuits, plural.

00:35:41.890 --> 00:35:44.150
One in the US, one in Japan.

00:35:44.150 --> 00:35:45.830
I get my cousin, Doug Houser,

00:35:45.830 --> 00:35:50.100
Stanford Law, 1960 to take our case on a
contingent fee.

00:35:50.100 --> 00:35:52.349
It took three years, we won both suits.

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:57.160
Meanwhile, we paid $35 to a graphic arts
student at Portland State College to

00:35:57.160 --> 00:36:01.070
give a rendition of the side logo on the
shoe, the logo now called the swoosh.

00:36:02.450 --> 00:36:04.810
Years later, she was interviewed by the
Portland Oregonian and

00:36:04.810 --> 00:36:07.100
asked her what her second biggest project
was.

00:36:07.100 --> 00:36:10.085
She answered, Walla, wallpaper for a Walla
Walla motel.

00:36:10.085 --> 00:36:11.094
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:36:12.802 --> 00:36:16.670
&gt;&gt; We had each of the 45 employees submit
a suggestion for a brand name.

00:36:16.670 --> 00:36:22.260
Jeff Johnson, 1963 Stanford graduate, who
I had met on a workout at Angel Field and

00:36:22.260 --> 00:36:25.420
who was our first employee, submitted the
name Nike.

00:36:26.550 --> 00:36:29.170
Well I said, I don't really like it that
much, but

00:36:29.170 --> 00:36:33.230
it's better than any of the other 44 and
hoped, hopefully it would grow on us.

00:36:35.020 --> 00:36:37.760
We were no longer limited to track and
field shoes.

00:36:37.760 --> 00:36:41.110
So we brought in wrestling shoes, tennis
shoes, basketball shoes as well.

00:36:41.110 --> 00:36:43.090
Sales grew to 3.2 million dollars.

00:36:43.090 --> 00:36:45.560
But our first, we had our first ever loss.

00:36:45.560 --> 00:36:46.830
Plus one other little problem.

00:36:46.830 --> 00:36:48.720
We got kicked out of our bank.

00:36:49.860 --> 00:36:52.300
Too much leverage, not enough cash.

00:36:52.300 --> 00:36:54.400
The state of Oregon only had two big
banks, and

00:36:54.400 --> 00:36:56.380
we'd been thrown out of the other one two
years before.

00:36:56.380 --> 00:36:58.930
[UNKNOWN]

00:36:58.930 --> 00:37:03.630
stood in for the bank until we found one,
The First State Bank of Milwaukee-Oregon.

00:37:03.630 --> 00:37:05.370
A small bank, but we made it work.

00:37:06.950 --> 00:37:07.670
So there I was,

00:37:07.670 --> 00:37:11.400
sitting at my desk, more relaxed than any
time in more than a decade.

00:37:11.400 --> 00:37:13.340
In the period between getting cut off by
Tiger and

00:37:13.340 --> 00:37:16.940
establishing a banking relationship with
the First State Bank of Milwaukie, Oregon.

00:37:16.940 --> 00:37:20.830
Sales merely through the introduction of a
running shoe with a waffle sole,

00:37:20.830 --> 00:37:25.260
had risen to $25 million with solid
profitability, profitability.

00:37:25.260 --> 00:37:26.450
Oh yes.

00:37:26.450 --> 00:37:31.670
Right at the top of the morning mail,
letterhead says, United States Customs.

00:37:31.670 --> 00:37:34.100
An announcement that this is an important
letter.

00:37:34.100 --> 00:37:36.030
It turned out to be so.

00:37:36.030 --> 00:37:40.720
Attached was an invoice for past through
custom duties of 25 million dollars.

00:37:41.810 --> 00:37:45.280
The exact same number as our total sales
for the year.

00:37:45.280 --> 00:37:46.770
I had no idea what they were talking
about.

00:37:46.770 --> 00:37:48.640
And so what we find out,

00:37:48.640 --> 00:37:52.890
there's a little use part of the customs
code dating back to the 1930s.

00:37:52.890 --> 00:37:57.940
Duty in three categories, benzedrine
chemicals, cherry stone clams and

00:37:57.940 --> 00:38:01.680
athletic footwear with synthetic uppers,
could be assessed.

00:38:01.680 --> 00:38:03.800
Not the factory cost of the goods but,

00:38:03.800 --> 00:38:07.390
on the American wholesale selling price of
those goods in in fact good were like or

00:38:07.390 --> 00:38:10.720
similar, if you like that language, to
American manufactured goods.

00:38:13.270 --> 00:38:17.010
So here comes this invoice, and in spite
of the fact that we'd been charged and

00:38:17.010 --> 00:38:20.100
paid the amounts that we had been
invoiced, for, we'd been invoiced for

00:38:20.100 --> 00:38:24.850
previously, our prices have been based on
that and long ago been sold.

00:38:24.850 --> 00:38:28.250
The $25 million was on top of that.

00:38:28.250 --> 00:38:30.760
But things were fuzzy and inclear, fuzzy
and unclear.

00:38:30.760 --> 00:38:32.660
One thing was absolutely certain.

00:38:32.660 --> 00:38:36.340
No way were we going to be able to pay
that amount of money, so

00:38:36.340 --> 00:38:39.490
I go to Washington, DC, reaching as high
up in the political ladder as I can.

00:38:39.490 --> 00:38:42.800
When first contemplating imported nylon
upper running shoes,

00:38:42.800 --> 00:38:45.000
we had asked Customs for a ruling on what
the duty would be.

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:48.000
When shown the letter signed by the
Assistant Director of

00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:52.980
Customs stating our duty would be 20% of
our factory cost, not the double that.

00:38:52.980 --> 00:38:55.190
That we were being assessed, the response
of John P.

00:38:55.190 --> 00:38:57.780
Simpson, Deputy Assistant Secretary.

00:38:57.780 --> 00:39:00.850
Well that letter is not binding on US
customs.

00:39:00.850 --> 00:39:02.870
In other words, we lied to you.

00:39:02.870 --> 00:39:03.770
You screwed up.

00:39:03.770 --> 00:39:04.530
You trusted us.

00:39:06.200 --> 00:39:08.320
And gradually we began to figure it out.

00:39:08.320 --> 00:39:11.990
This obscure rule had been on the books
for nearly half a century.

00:39:11.990 --> 00:39:16.230
And now US shoe manufacturers, Converse
and others, banded together to

00:39:16.230 --> 00:39:19.820
lobby the government to ply the extra duty
on athletic shoe imports and

00:39:19.820 --> 00:39:21.540
general and us in particular.

00:39:22.710 --> 00:39:25.890
They had to make something that met the
test of a customs officer who likely had

00:39:25.890 --> 00:39:29.390
never worked in a shoe factory, as in like
or similar.

00:39:29.390 --> 00:39:32.820
And they then had to sell only a few of
those shoes to hugely increase the cost of

00:39:32.820 --> 00:39:34.010
our shoes going forward if,

00:39:34.010 --> 00:39:37.920
in fact, the retroactive part didn't put
us out of business, which it nearly did.

00:39:38.970 --> 00:39:42.860
Thus began the great ASP fight, a fight
for our very lives.

00:39:43.870 --> 00:39:45.520
It lasted three years.

00:39:45.520 --> 00:39:48.819
I am in a secret business and I've got to
have a Washington D.C. lobby.

00:39:50.110 --> 00:39:54.520
Most lobbying firms on K street were more
than willing to take our case for

00:39:54.520 --> 00:39:57.200
a fee of $1,000 an hour.

00:39:57.200 --> 00:40:01.790
We hired Jay Edwards, Stanford '68, he had
just opened an office representing

00:40:01.790 --> 00:40:06.690
Portland General Electric, the Oregon
public utility and the Nez Perce indians.

00:40:06.690 --> 00:40:09.800
And now for a retainer of $300 a month.

00:40:09.800 --> 00:40:15.250
Us, any over, around and through, we
fought like bastards.

00:40:15.250 --> 00:40:18.750
Our cause was just, the government was
aligned with the forces of evil, and

00:40:18.750 --> 00:40:21.270
if we lost, if we lost, we were kaput.

00:40:22.580 --> 00:40:26.180
But I believe that fight made a huge
imprint on our culture,

00:40:26.180 --> 00:40:27.130
which lasts to this day.

00:40:28.280 --> 00:40:31.105
We joined the American footwear
manufacturers and then sued them.

00:40:31.105 --> 00:40:35.250
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:40:35.250 --> 00:40:37.670
&gt;&gt; We made an inflammatory TV commercial,

00:40:37.670 --> 00:40:42.160
which ended with the tag line, if this
little shoe company goes out of business,

00:40:42.160 --> 00:40:44.285
a little bit of Yankee freedom dies with
us.

00:40:44.285 --> 00:40:46.000
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:40:46.000 --> 00:40:48.462
&gt;&gt; No reputable TV network or channel
would show it.

00:40:48.462 --> 00:40:51.320
[COUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:40:51.320 --> 00:40:54.320
&gt;&gt; We got on the air on a New England
religious channel between 12 and

00:40:54.320 --> 00:40:55.345
one in the morning.

00:40:55.345 --> 00:40:58.610
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:40:58.610 --> 00:41:01.680
&gt;&gt; It generated three letters, all of them
positive.

00:41:03.860 --> 00:41:04.940
That having failed,

00:41:04.940 --> 00:41:10.220
we took a portable tv set, showing the ad
during the presidential campaign of 1976,

00:41:10.220 --> 00:41:15.760
showing it in cafeterias, diners, and
pizza parlors all over New Hampshire.

00:41:15.760 --> 00:41:18.840
And that managed to get the attention of
the political establishment,

00:41:18.840 --> 00:41:19.670
at least a little bit.

00:41:19.670 --> 00:41:24.310
And there was lots of shoe leather in
D.C., including plenty of my own.

00:41:24.310 --> 00:41:27.340
We had support from the Oregon delegation,
plus Al Gore and

00:41:27.340 --> 00:41:30.520
Jim Sasser of Tennessee, where we had our
central warehouse.

00:41:30.520 --> 00:41:32.810
In one of our meetings at treasury,

00:41:32.810 --> 00:41:37.080
the official said, you can tell your
Senator Hatfield to quit calling.

00:41:37.080 --> 00:41:38.420
It's not doing you any good.

00:41:39.620 --> 00:41:40.470
I left his office and

00:41:40.470 --> 00:41:42.682
called Mark Hidfeld's office and said,
keep up the good work.

00:41:42.682 --> 00:41:44.046
[COUGH]

00:41:44.046 --> 00:41:45.410
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:41:45.410 --> 00:41:49.885
&gt;&gt; Our one in house lawyer, Rich Warskill,
Stanford '68.

00:41:49.885 --> 00:41:52.510
Lived for two years in Washington, DC.

00:41:52.510 --> 00:41:58.370
He and Jay Edwards simply outworked,
out-thought, out-emotioned the opposition,

00:41:58.370 --> 00:42:03.490
and did a better job in this case than any
of those K Street lawyers would have.

00:42:03.490 --> 00:42:07.110
In perhaps our best maneuver, we came up
with this one.

00:42:07.110 --> 00:42:11.560
We had a factory in Exeter, New Hampshire,
making 15,000 pair a month.

00:42:11.560 --> 00:42:14.350
What if we created a second line, knocked
off

00:42:14.350 --> 00:42:19.480
ourselves selling the discounters a very
low but marginally profitable price.

00:42:19.480 --> 00:42:23.490
No one could copy us closer than we could
copy ourselves.

00:42:23.490 --> 00:42:27.480
When the first came up in a brainstorming
session everybody laughed at its

00:42:27.480 --> 00:42:30.670
absurdity, then we looked at each other.

00:42:30.670 --> 00:42:31.750
The whole law was absurd.

00:42:33.150 --> 00:42:36.330
And it evolved into eventually let's try
it.

00:42:37.580 --> 00:42:40.530
Thus was born the one line, which over a
couple of years,

00:42:40.530 --> 00:42:46.120
sold a couple thousand pairs and reduced
the increase in our duties by two thirds.

00:42:46.120 --> 00:42:49.689
And after three years of fighting, we
settled the great ASP customs battle.

00:42:50.840 --> 00:42:54.550
For $9 million, approximately one third of
the former demand.

00:42:54.550 --> 00:42:57.600
And in those three years our sales had
grown to $140 million and

00:42:57.600 --> 00:43:01.540
we could actually pay the bill.

00:43:01.540 --> 00:43:05.200
One year after the settlement we got ASP
eliminated from all,

00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:09.090
from the entire US customs clode, code,
from benzedine chemicals and

00:43:09.090 --> 00:43:13.020
cherrystone clamps, as well as footwear
with synthetic uppers.

00:43:13.020 --> 00:43:16.470
While it would reach the critical mass to
go public all during the ASP years we

00:43:16.470 --> 00:43:19.780
could not go public because we could not
accurately report earnings

00:43:19.780 --> 00:43:23.990
which were very materially affected by the
ultimate ASP resolution.

00:43:25.070 --> 00:43:30.950
With the resolution of ASP a public
offering was open to us,

00:43:30.950 --> 00:43:32.310
and in December 1980.

00:43:32.310 --> 00:43:33.930
We did just that.

00:43:33.930 --> 00:43:38.110
And from that point, the only thing
standing in the way of real success,

00:43:38.110 --> 00:43:41.100
of having our dreams come true, was
ourselves.

00:43:43.150 --> 00:43:47.000
I don't really like lessons learned type
lectures.

00:43:47.000 --> 00:43:48.870
But there have been a few along the way.

00:43:50.040 --> 00:43:52.280
And on this special occasion, I can't help
myself.

00:43:53.280 --> 00:43:55.695
Indulge me, I'll not be doing this again.

00:43:55.695 --> 00:43:58.330
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:43:58.330 --> 00:44:03.960
&gt;&gt; The goal should not be to seek a job or
even a career but to seek a calling.

00:44:03.960 --> 00:44:05.610
That search has just begun.

00:44:05.610 --> 00:44:07.750
Now that you have grad,

00:44:09.010 --> 00:44:12.590
now that you have graduated the goal
should not be to seek a job.

00:44:12.590 --> 00:44:17.969
Or even a career but, you should be, seek
a calling.

00:44:17.969 --> 00:44:21.970
In your time here, you've probably gone
through 50 or 100 different case studies.

00:44:21.970 --> 00:44:24.990
And in the years ahead, you'll probably go
through thousands more.

00:44:24.990 --> 00:44:27.700
Those case studies are not about decision
making,

00:44:27.700 --> 00:44:31.090
not even about judgement, they are about a
search for wisdom.

00:44:32.540 --> 00:44:35.500
I have in my travels, occasionally, met
promising young people who

00:44:35.500 --> 00:44:38.610
insist they're not going to ask for help
along the way.

00:44:38.610 --> 00:44:40.110
They want to figure it out themselves.

00:44:42.150 --> 00:44:44.020
Mine was the opposite approach.

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:45.770
It is hard enough out there.

00:44:45.770 --> 00:44:47.650
Get all the help you can.

00:44:47.650 --> 00:44:51.479
Getting help really is just a part of a
life long search for wisdom.

00:44:51.479 --> 00:44:56.021
Here are just a few of the people who
stooped to help us along the way, and

00:44:56.021 --> 00:45:01.272
all but one of the following began their
help before we were ever a public company.

00:45:01.272 --> 00:45:05.512
Chuck Robinson, Deputy Secretary of State
to Henry Kissinger.

00:45:05.512 --> 00:45:09.039
Jill Conway, President of Smith College,
Dick Donahue,

00:45:09.039 --> 00:45:13.960
at 28-years-old, a congressional liaison
for John Kennedy.

00:45:13.960 --> 00:45:19.200
Ralph Denuncio, president of the New York
Stock Exchange, Tom Paine,

00:45:19.200 --> 00:45:20.870
the Executive Director of NASA.

00:45:22.100 --> 00:45:25.980
Masaru Hayami, the president of Nissho
Iwai.

00:45:25.980 --> 00:45:27.460
As we increased our volume,

00:45:27.460 --> 00:45:31.740
we became Nissho Iwai's most profitable
customer in the world.

00:45:31.740 --> 00:45:34.060
Which got me into meetings with their CVO,
CEO.

00:45:35.400 --> 00:45:38.430
We developed a friendship, and for five
years in a row,

00:45:38.430 --> 00:45:42.430
I spent I weekend with him and his wife at
their beach cabin near Atami.

00:45:42.430 --> 00:45:46.220
Which wasn't just a pleasant weekend it
was also a tutorial

00:45:46.220 --> 00:45:47.380
on business and leadership.

00:45:48.820 --> 00:45:53.070
Two years after he stepped down from
Nisho, Masaru Huyami was named

00:45:53.070 --> 00:45:56.860
the governor of the Bank of Japan which is
the Federal Reserve of Japan.

00:45:57.870 --> 00:46:01.520
When he passed away, at his memorial
service in Tokyo.

00:46:01.520 --> 00:46:05.429
Per his instructions, draped over his
coffin was a Nike banner.

00:46:05.429 --> 00:46:13.881
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:46:13.881 --> 00:46:16.320
And don't be afraid to come back to the
school that spawned you.

00:46:17.430 --> 00:46:20.130
Frank Schalanburger always raised my
spirits when,

00:46:20.130 --> 00:46:23.080
when I was feeling low and wanted to get
ahold of him.

00:46:23.080 --> 00:46:28.770
Bob Davis and Mike Spence each spent ten
years on the Nike Board of Directors.

00:46:28.770 --> 00:46:30.900
And there are a couple other lessons.

00:46:30.900 --> 00:46:34.500
Two nines working together will beat two
tens working for

00:46:34.500 --> 00:46:36.270
their own careers, every time.

00:46:37.470 --> 00:46:41.570
Ability and desire most always trump money
and power.

00:46:42.730 --> 00:46:46.780
If you can't get financing, don't be
afraid to go 7,000 miles from home.

00:46:47.970 --> 00:46:51.630
Government is part of business, any
business.

00:46:51.630 --> 00:46:57.720
There is such a thing as managing
creativity, and dare to take chances,

00:46:57.720 --> 00:46:59.970
lest you leave your talent buried in the
ground.

00:47:01.180 --> 00:47:03.140
And where there is no struggle.

00:47:03.140 --> 00:47:04.070
There can be no art.

00:47:05.190 --> 00:47:10.340
And finally, there's this thought, ten
years from now

00:47:10.340 --> 00:47:13.790
the first of you will be asked to give the
commencement speech to what will then be

00:47:13.790 --> 00:47:16.590
the finest class in the school's history.

00:47:18.330 --> 00:47:20.050
You'll be a bit torn.

00:47:20.050 --> 00:47:22.520
You will multitask to the max.

00:47:22.520 --> 00:47:23.700
Two kids.

00:47:23.700 --> 00:47:27.320
One has an ear infection and needs to get
to the doctor right away.

00:47:27.320 --> 00:47:29.940
Your husband is more needy than usual and

00:47:29.940 --> 00:47:32.940
he has a flight in the morning to Europe
for ten days.

00:47:32.940 --> 00:47:36.250
Your company is at a critical point in
its' strategic planning and

00:47:36.250 --> 00:47:39.930
everybody looks to you for what the
answers will be.

00:47:39.930 --> 00:47:41.990
Plus the company has a PR crisis, and

00:47:41.990 --> 00:47:45.640
you have TV appearances scheduled for five
days straight.

00:47:45.640 --> 00:47:47.500
And that Golden Lab you had for

00:47:47.500 --> 00:47:50.530
two years, has all of a sudden decided
it's not housebroken.

00:47:52.150 --> 00:47:56.330
There is not time, there is no time, and
then you'll accept.

00:47:57.620 --> 00:47:59.440
Because of the honor.

00:47:59.440 --> 00:48:03.040
Because it's a chance to have some
influence on the most able,

00:48:03.040 --> 00:48:04.820
best prepared young people on the planet.

00:48:06.100 --> 00:48:11.290
And you will accept though it is hard to
see now, because there's a part of you

00:48:11.290 --> 00:48:16.800
that longs to go back to a place and a
time and a self forever gone.

00:48:18.090 --> 00:48:20.240
In looking for things to say,

00:48:20.240 --> 00:48:24.970
include in your consideration moments from
the school's history.

00:48:24.970 --> 00:48:28.210
You might even look back to that time from
the deep past,

00:48:28.210 --> 00:48:32.140
that moment from over six decades before
when Frank Shellenberger,

00:48:32.140 --> 00:48:36.680
beloved professor of entrepreneurship,
said the words that meant so much to me.

00:48:36.680 --> 00:48:40.500
The words that became the mantra for his
class, the words that

00:48:40.500 --> 00:48:48.245
said the only time you must not fail is
the last time you try.

00:48:48.245 --> 00:48:58.245
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE].

00:49:28.876 --> 00:49:33.990
&gt;&gt; Thank you Phil for those inspiring and
dare I say, most memorable words.

00:49:33.990 --> 00:49:36.600
We thank you for all that you've done for
the school over the years.

00:49:37.640 --> 00:49:40.623
It is now my great honor and

00:49:40.623 --> 00:49:47.415
pleasure to introduce you to the GSB PhD
class of 2014.

00:49:47.415 --> 00:49:57.052
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE].

00:49:57.052 --> 00:50:01.140
&gt;&gt; Our PhD program is small and exclusive.

00:50:01.140 --> 00:50:03.540
With an admission rate well under 10%.

00:50:03.540 --> 00:50:09.570
These individuals that are about to come
before you come here as students,

00:50:09.570 --> 00:50:14.600
dedicating themselves to two or three
years of course work in an intensive

00:50:14.600 --> 00:50:19.100
apprenticeship in conducting research in
their chosen fields.

00:50:19.100 --> 00:50:21.420
Having become true experts.

00:50:21.420 --> 00:50:27.220
They leave us as colleagues, setting out
on a path to create new knowledge.

00:50:27.220 --> 00:50:31.840
Today we formally celebrate their
transition from students to

00:50:31.840 --> 00:50:36.630
future intellectual leaders who will play
an important role in shaping our

00:50:36.630 --> 00:50:40.130
understanding of business and management.

00:50:40.130 --> 00:50:44.660
After their advisors placed the hood of
the Stanford Business School on them.

00:50:44.660 --> 00:50:50.085
They will join us on stage, as we welcome
them into our profession, as colleagues.

